{"sections":[],"schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"patch":0,"major":0},"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/ArchitectureGuide","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux"]]},"kind":"article","metadata":{"modules":[{"name":"Swidux"}],"role":"article","title":"Architecture & Performance","roleHeading":"Article"},"abstract":[{"text":"Understand the architectural patterns that make Swidux work correctly with SwiftUI’s observation system.","type":"text"}],"seeAlsoSections":[{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/PluginArchitecture","doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/DesignPrinciples"],"generated":true,"anchor":"Explanation","title":"Explanation"}],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"content","content":[{"anchor":"Overview","text":"Overview","type":"heading","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Swidux relies on specific patterns to ensure correct "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Observable"},{"type":"text","text":" behavior, efficient rendering, and safe concurrency. This article explains the reasoning behind these patterns."}]},{"anchor":"The-Snapshot-Pattern","text":"The Snapshot Pattern","type":"heading","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Store.send()"},{"type":"text","text":" copies the observer tree into a local struct, mutates the copy via the reducer, then assigns changed properties back. This happens internally via "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"SwiduxObservable"},{"type":"text","text":" — "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"State(observer:)"},{"type":"text","text":" packs, "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"State.apply(_:to:)"},{"type":"text","text":" unpacks."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The pattern exists for two reasons:"}]},{"items":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Observable"},{"type":"text","text":" equality checking."}]},{"type":"text","text":" The "},{"code":"set","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" accessor checks "},{"code":"Equatable","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" and suppresses no-op notifications. Swift’s "},{"code":"_modify","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" accessor (used by "},{"code":"inout","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":") fires notifications unconditionally. The snapshot pattern routes through "},{"code":"set","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Cross-slice observation isolation."}]},{"type":"text","text":" The "},{"code":"@Swidux","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" macro generates a separate "},{"code":"@Observable","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" class for each "},{"code":"@Slice","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" property. A view reading "},{"code":"store.items","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" won’t re-render when only "},{"code":"store.ui","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" changes, because they live on different observer objects."}]}]}],"type":"orderedList"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Swidux"},{"type":"text","text":" macro generates the observer class tree and "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"SwiduxObservable"},{"type":"text","text":" conformance automatically. Hand-written conformance is still possible for advanced cases — see "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/SwiduxObservable"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"style":"note","type":"aside","name":"Note","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Explicit equality guards ("},{"code":"if x != state.x { x = state.x }","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":") are unnecessary. "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Observable"},{"type":"text","text":" already checks equality on "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"set"},{"type":"text","text":". Unconditional assignment is safe."}]}]},{"type":"heading","level":2,"anchor":"Dispatch-Loop-Detection","text":"Dispatch Loop Detection"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/PersistencePlugin","isActive":true},{"text":" warns if ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"afterReduce"},{"text":" is called more than 100 times per debounce interval. This usually means ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"send()"},{"text":" isn’t using the snapshot pattern, causing cascading re-renders that trigger re-dispatches.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","text":"Reducer Weight","level":2,"anchor":"Reducer-Weight"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Reducers run synchronously on the MainActor. Move O(n²) work into an "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/Effect"},{"text":" and dispatch the result back as an action.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","level":2,"anchor":"Effect-Threading","text":"Effect Threading"},{"name":"Important","type":"aside","style":"important","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Effects run on Swift concurrency’s cooperative thread pool via "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Task { @concurrent in }"},{"type":"text","text":". Blocking calls ("},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Process.waitUntilExit()"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"DispatchSemaphore.wait()"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Thread.sleep()"},{"text":") hold threads hostage. If enough block, the pool starves and the MainActor freezes.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Use async alternatives: ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"terminationHandler"},{"text":" + continuation instead of ","type":"text"},{"code":"waitU